  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1050 PM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nAn upper level low will be near the Four Corners area by 12z Wed and \nthen track slowly northeast into nern CO by 12z Thu.  The overall \ntrack of the low is a bit further north than what was shown last \nnight. However, there will be rather strong QG ascent associated \nwith the system along with favorable lapse rates and plenty of \nmoisture. Overall, will continue to see rounds of showers through \nWed aftn with snow levels lowering to 8000 ft in the aftn. Highs \non Wed will stay mainly in the 40's across most of nern CO.\n\nFor Wed night, as the upper level low moves closer to the front \nrange, may see a decrease in precipitation along portions of the I-\n25 Corridor and over the higher terrain along and south of I-70 by\nmid evening, while precipitation continues over the plains and \nnrn areas of the foothills and mtns. By 12z Thu, the upper level \nlow will be over nern CO and then move into swrn NE by early Thu \naftn. Meanwhile, another surge of cooler air will move across the \nplains with northerly low level flow. This feature, combined with \nmoisture wrapping around the upper level low, may lead to a round\nof rain along portions of the I-25 Corridor and plains thru the \nmorning hours. By aftn, the upper level low will gradually weaken\nas it continues to move east. Thus should see a gradual decrease \nin precipitation thru the aftn hours. As for highs, readings will \nbe in the 40's to mid 50's across the plains.\n\nBy Thu night into Fri an upper level high will build over the area. \nThis will lead to dry conditions with moderating temperatures. \nMeanwhile, the first freeze of the season still looks likely over \nmost of the plains late Thu night into Fri morning.  \n\nFor the weekend, a weak upper level disturbance will move across \nlate Sat into early Sun.  However, cross-sections continue to show \nlimited moisture with this feature.  Thus expect dry conditions to \ncontinue as temps warm back to above seasonal normals.\n\nLooking ahead to early next week, the low aloft will become \nsouthwesterly as an upper level trough develops over the wrn US.  As \na result, this will lead to dry conditions with a continuation of \nabove normal temperatures.",
